WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.540
Avengers: Endgame provided a defining moment
for Scarlett Johansson's Natasha Romanoff,

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:10.200
AKA Black Widow, one which caused a stir among
many MCU fans.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:15.059
In an Entertainment Weekly interview, directors
Joe and Anthony Russo felt the need to clarify

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:19.200
their thinking in including the scene, which
one could hardly argue was out of character

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:20.320
for Romanoff.

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:23.260
Be advised that spoilers for Endgame follow.

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:28.280
During the Avengers' "Time Heist," in which
separate teams of heroes were sent to different

00:00:28.289 --> 00:00:33.010
periods of time in order to retrieve the six
Infinity Stones long before Thanos could get

00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:37.830
his massive purple hands on them, Romanoff
and longtime partner in crime Clint Barton

00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:44.000
(AKA Hawkeye, AKA Ronin) are dispatched to
the planet Vormir to secure the Soul Stone.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:48.220
The pair meet the ghostly Red Skull, who informs
them that they would have to deal with the

00:00:48.230 --> 00:00:53.330
same quandary faced by Thanos in Infinity
War: in order to possess the stone, one must

00:00:53.330 --> 00:00:54.940
give up that which they love.

00:00:54.940 --> 00:00:59.860
Realizing the ramifications of the situation,
the old friends proceed to fight to the death,

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:04.040
that is, for the right to be the one to go
over the cliff, making the ultimate sacrifice

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:06.200
so that the other might complete the mission.

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:11.260
In the end, Romanoff plunges to her doom,
leaving Barton in possession of the stone

00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:15.570
and faced with the unenviable task of returning
to the present day to inform the Avengers

00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:17.420
that Romanoff was no more.

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:22.900
As New York Times pop culture reporter Kyle
Buchanan noted on Twitter, Vormir's not exactly

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:24.860
a great planet for redheads.

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:28.720
"A soul for a soul."

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:34.620
It was a sacrifice only fitting for a character
who had a lot of red in her ledger, and was

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:40.040
driven by a need to wipe it out, but some
fans saw it as an unfortunate example of "fridging,"

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:44.920
a trope in which a female character's death
is used to drive the motivations of male characters.

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:49.880
The trope gets its name from an issue of Green
Lantern, in which Kyle Rayner literally discovers

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:52.760
his deceased girlfriend stuffed into his refrigerator.

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:58.299
In their interview, the Russos defended their
decision by bringing up multiple points, first

00:01:58.299 --> 00:02:02.350
and foremost, the fact that in an all-out
brawl between Black Widow and Hawkeye, the

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:06.840
eventual victor would necessarily be determined
not just by who wanted it the most, but by

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:08.480
who was the most skilled.

00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:10.060
Joe Russo explained,

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:23.940
But that was far from the only factor.

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:28.400
In defending their choice, the directors explained
that in essence, Romanoff was motivated by

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:33.100
her desire to see her friend redeem himself
after his stint as the bloodthirsty Ronin,

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:37.160
and reunite with his lost family, who had
all been dusted in the Decimation.

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:39.220
Anthony Russo told EW,

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:58.940
Joe Russo continued to relate that Widow's
choice plays into the larger theme of Endgame,

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:01.800
which is the opposite of that explored in
Infinity War.

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:25.180
The director continued to say that this specific
theme of sacrifice was appropriate to Romanoff's

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:30.120
character arc, going from a mercenary-style
loner to a full-fledged protector who sees

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:34.560
her survival as being secondary to the goal
of the Avengers, the only real family she's

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:38.060
ever known, to save the universe itself from
untold destruction.

00:03:49.460 --> 00:03:55.339
It's an idea explored in multiple Marvel Cinematic
Universe offerings, from Steve Rogers' willingness

00:03:55.339 --> 00:03:59.680
to face certain death at the conclusion of
Captain America: The First Avenger to Vision's

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:03.790
insistence on sacrificing himself so that
Thanos would be thwarted in his attempt to

00:04:03.790 --> 00:04:06.260
possess the Mind Stone in Infinity War.

00:04:06.260 --> 00:04:11.120
Black Widow fans always knew she'd go down
fighting, and she did, not against the forces

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:15.559
of evil, but against her most beloved ally,
for the right to be the one to die so that

00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:19.160
the other might be able to live in peace for
perhaps the first time.

